Toby <toby@nospam.fakeaddress.com> wrote in message news:<20040417105221681+0100@news.ox.ac.uk>...

> I mustn't have made myself clear. Even for first time players who have 
> had years of trouble, we often find out that they just got lucky with an 
> early wand and won in a great anticlimax. I think that this is really 
> disappointing and should be remedied.

As one of those recent YAFAPs who benefitted from an early /oW, I just 
want to say: bite me.  You may find my ascension "a great anticlimax", 
but I sure didn't.  Sure it was a very lucky break to find a /oW shortly 
after Sokoban, but it didn't guarantee anything.  I (and I'm sure I'm 
not the only one) have blown a couple other games with a random /oW in 
the past 2 years; I have also had very good games without one.  My 
ascension character could have still met YAAD, just as easily as one of 
those YAADs without the wand could have been my YAFAP.

I very much appreciated the random wand, was not disappointed to find 
it, and don't feel like it took away from the experience of ascending.  
Now that I have an ascension under my belt, I am pretty confident that 
if I can get another good game going, I can do it again without the 
random wand.  But for now at least, should I find one, I will happily 
use it, with no regrets, even if it enables me to ascend.  Perhaps 
eventually I will get to where I will decline the extra help, but 
NetHack is a damn hard game, with or without the wand, and I'm not at 
that point yet.

> Conducts do not do this as such 
> players who haven't yet ascended (the YAFAP's I mentioned) will not use 
> them.

And why would I want to?  "Gee, 2 years of frustration isn't enough.  I 
think I'll just use this wand 3 times, so on the off-chance I ascend, it 
won't be anticlimatic."

> Also, they really break the flow of any conduct game that doesn't 
> include wishless. For example, if I play something quite tough like a 
> Vegetarian Illiterate Tourist, then get an early wand the game becomes 
> quite trivial if I use all the wishes, but would be OK if I could only 
> get a few.

It's part of the game, and it's not a bug, so it's your prerogative to 
use it, use some of it, or discard it.  Just like it's your prerogative 
to look down your nose at a vegetarian illiterate tourist (or YAFAP) who 
uses 14 wishes.

> Let's look at it another way. Would you be in favour of increasing the 
> number of wishes to 8 or how about 14? if not, and you aren't in favour 
> of decreasing them either, then what is it about 7 that is so perfect?
> 
> What if there were 14 wishes from a wand? Would you just say that this 
> is fine and conducts solve the problem? How is the case of 7 wishes 
> relevantly different?

Well, you've got to draw the line somewhere, and the DevTeam did it at 
7.  I don't think a game that takes most people many months or even 
years to win is too easy.  If it's too easy for you, feel free to draw 
the line where you want in your games.

